# Appendix E: the X11 version.
.doc Strata Reference: The X11 Version
.chapter E
.h1 The X11 Version [appx11]
.ix Appendix E
.ix X11 version
.ix X Window System
.ix Window, X11
This appendix describes the X11 version of the Editor and the Document
Manager, and the Viewer that goes with it: the window, the fonts, the
colors, the keyboard and the trouble that a keyboard map or a window manager
can make, the size of the window, moving text between programs, and the
Viewer.  The terminal version is described in {n:appterm}, and
{x:view-screen-kinds} lists the differences that matter in daily
work.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
The environment variables of the window are listed with all the others
in {x:appcmd-env-x11}; this appendix says what they do.
.endif

The sections follow what a user meets when the window opens: what the machine
must have ({n:appx11-needs}); the window, its fonts, its colors and its size
({n:appx11-window}, {n:appx11-fonts}, {n:appx11-colour}, {n:appx11-size});
the keyboard and the faults that a keyboard map or a window manager can cause
({n:appx11-keys}, {n:appx11-keymap}); how text moves between programs
({n:appx11-clip}); the pictures that the window shows ({n:appx11-pictures});
and last the Viewer, which has a window of its own ({n:appx11-viewer}).  The
window is a grid of character cells, like the screen of the terminal version,
so the Editor and the Document Manager offer the same commands.  The window
adds the following.
- Alt, Ctrl and Shift arrive as they are: every key that a PC keyboard has
  can be used.
- The key labels at the foot of the screen follow the modifier keys.
- The Equation, Picture, Keycap and LaTeX markers whose display mode is Inline
  are drawn as pictures, when LaTeX is installed ({n:appx11-pictures}).
- The text has bold and italic faces, and any color that you choose for
  every kind of text ({n:appx11-colour}).
- The mouse wheel scrolls the text, and a click puts the cursor where the
  mouse arrow is ({n:appx11-mouse}).

.h2 What the X11 version needs [appx11-needs]
.ix X11, requirements
.ix DISPLAY
.ix Xft
.ix X display
.table 16 44
Need | Detail
A display | The environment variable {c:DISPLAY} names the X server; the program
  | opens it at start.  When that fails, the program writes
  | {c:needs an interactive terminal (or an X display with the x11 build)}
  | on the standard error and ends.  It never
  | falls back to a terminal.
A font | A monospaced font: an outline font (the family DejaVu Sans Mono
  | is the first choice) or a bitmap font of the server
  | ({n:appx11-fonts}).  If none can be opened the program ends as above.
LaTeX programs | Optional.  Equations are typeset on the screen when {c:latex}
  | and {c:dvipng} are in the system directories for programs at the
  | time the program starts ({n:eq-screen}).  Pictures, keycaps and
  | LaTeX markers need {c:pdflatex} and {c:pdftoppm}.  Without
  | them these markers are shown in their Short form.  The Viewer
  | needs more ({n:appx11-viewer}).
.end

.h2 The window [appx11-window]
.ix Window, size
.ix Window title
.ix WM_CLASS
.ix Window class
.ix Window, close
The program opens one window and draws everything in it: the status line, the
text, the menus and the boxes are the cells of one grid.
.table 11 49
Matter | What is so
Size | 80 columns by 50 rows of character cells at first
  | ({n:appx11-size}).  You may change the height of the window,
  | in whole rows, and it is kept for the next time; the width
  | cannot be changed.
Position | The window manager places the window.
Title | The name of the program that owns the window (the Editor and
  | the Document Manager each set their own).  It does not show
  | the name of the document, and it does not change while you
  | work.
Class | The class is {c:Strata}; the instance is the same for the Editor
  | and the Document Manager.  Use the class for the rules of a window
  | manager.
Close | Closing the window with the window manager acts as {k:Ctrl-Q}:
  | if the document has unsaved changes a prompt offers "Save and quit",
  | "Discard changes and quit" or "Cancel", and the window stays when you
  | choose Cancel or press {k:Esc}.  An unchanged document ends at once.
.end
Two programs of Strata can have a window at the same time.  The Document
Manager keeps its window while the Editor that it started has a window of its
own: they lie side by side and are windows of the same kind.  The same holds
for the Editor and the Viewer of Preview.  A window that waits for the
program that it started repaints itself but takes no keys: keys that are
pressed in it meanwhile are lost.

.h2 Fonts [appx11-fonts]
.ix Fonts of the screen
.ix Xft
.ix DejaVu Sans Mono
.ix Bitmap fonts
.ix Cell size
All text is drawn on a grid.  The width of a cell is that of the letter M of
the font, and its height is the ascent and the descent of the font together.
Letters are placed on the cells one by one, so a font that is not monospaced
does not break the grid, but it is spaced unevenly.  The program looks for a
font in two steps.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.ix STRATA_X11_FONT
.ix STRATA_X11_XFT
.ix STRATA_X11_SIZE
.table 10 50
Step | What it does
1 Outline | Unless {c:STRATA_X11_XFT} is {c:0}.  The family is the value
  | of {c:STRATA_X11_XFT}, or DejaVu Sans Mono when it
  | is not set, is empty or is {c:1}.  The pixel size is
  | {c:STRATA_X11_SIZE} (16 when it is not set, not a number or
  | less than 8).  Four faces are used: normal, bold,
  | italic and bold italic. A face whose M is not as wide as
  | that of the normal face is dropped (bold is then shown
  | by its color only, italic by its color). A letter that the
  | font lacks is taken from the family DejaVu Sans at the
  | same size, if it is installed.  If the normal face cannot
  | be used, or is smaller than 4 by 6 pixels, step 2
  | follows.
2 Bitmap | The fonts of the server.  The name in
  | {c:STRATA_X11_FONT} is the full name of the normal font; the
  | bold font has the same name with the word {c:medium}
  | replaced by {c:bold}.  When it is not set the
  | names are
  | {c:-misc-fixed-medium-r-normal--N-...-iso10646-1}, with
  | the size N from {c:STRATA_X11_SIZE}: 13, 15, 18 or 20 (any
  | other value gives 15).  If the font cannot be opened, the
  | 15 pixel font is tried, and then the font named
  | {c:fixed}.  If none opens, the program ends as in
  | {x:appx11-needs}. A bold font that is not as wide as the
  | normal one is not used.
.end
The font that {c:STRATA_X11_FONT} names must be of the encoding {c:iso10646-1},
or
the box drawing characters of the screen are drawn with wrong shapes.  The
environment variable {c:STRATA_X11_FONT} is read only in step 2: it has no
effect
unless {c:STRATA_X11_XFT} is {c:0}, or the outline font cannot be used.
.else
.table 10 50
Step | What it does
1 Outline | The family is DejaVu Sans Mono.  The pixel size is 16.  Four
  | faces are used: normal, bold, italic and bold italic. A face whose M is
  | not as wide as that of the normal face is dropped (bold is then shown
  | by its color only, italic by its color). A letter that the
  | font lacks is taken from the family DejaVu Sans at the
  | same size, if it is installed.  If the normal face cannot
  | be used, or is smaller than 4 by 6 pixels, step 2
  | follows.
2 Bitmap | The fonts of the server.  The names are
  | {c:-misc-fixed-medium-r-normal--15-...-iso10646-1} for the normal font
  | and the same with the word {c:medium} replaced by {c:bold} for the bold
  | one.  If the font cannot be opened, the font named
  | {c:fixed} is tried.  If none opens, the program ends as in
  | {x:appx11-needs}. A bold font that is not as wide as the
  | normal one is not used.
.end
.endif

If the family of the outline font is not installed, the font system supplies
the nearest font that it has.  When that is not monospaced, the letters are
still put on the cells but not evenly.  Name a monospaced family that is
installed.
.note Bold text is drawn in the color of bold text and in the bold face; an
  italic text is drawn in the color of italic text and in the italic face
  when there is one ({n:appx11-colour}).  A face that is dropped because of
  its width is replaced by the normal face, and only the color shows the
  style.

.h2 Colors [appx11-colour]
.ix Colors, X11
.ix Palette
.ix Cursor, X11
.ix Reverse video, X11
.ix Keycap, box
.ix Underline, X11
The colors of the window are those of the Colors panel of Setup
({n:str-colours}) for the X11 version: yellow text on a blue ground, until you
change them.  The Editor and the Document Manager both use them, and the
Viewer has its own.  Any color can be chosen: the palette of the panel holds
80, and a name or {c:#rrggbb} typed in gives any other.  The settings are
x11_color_KIND_fg and x11_color_KIND_bg ({n:appcfg-key-colors}), apart from
those of the terminal version, and a change is shown at once.
.table 18 17 24
Use | Colors at first | Notes
Normal text | Yellow on blue | The text of a document, and the page.
Bold text | White on blue | Also the bold face.
Italic text | Light green on blue | Also the italic face, when there is
  |  | one.
Dim text | Light grey on blue | The dots for spaces, the signs of
  |  | tabs and line ends, and the ruler.
Markers | Light cyan on blue | The text of markers in the line.
Highlight | Black on cyan | The area that Copy, Cut, Delete or Move
  |  | acts on.
Status line and others | Black on light grey | The status line, the
  |  | message line, the key labels, menus and
  |  | panels.
Selected entry | White on black | The entry that has the focus.
Cursor | Black on amber | A solid block with its character in
  |  | black.
.end
The page color of an equation in the text is the color of normal text, and
its ink the foreground of normal text, so that an equation follows the colors
that you choose.  Underlined text has a line one pixel below the baseline,
in the color of its text.  A Keycap marker is drawn as a picture of a box of
thin lines with round corners, in the colors of the Keycap Format
({n:marker-keycap}).  Where a picture is on the screen (an equation, a
picture, a keycap or a LaTeX marker) the cursor is drawn as a frame around the
picture.

.h2 The keyboard [appx11-keys]
.ix Keyboard, X11
.ix Keys, X11
.ix Modifier keys, X11
.ix Keypad, X11
.ix Alt, X11
.ix Num Lock
The window receives the keys of the keyboard as the keyboard map defines
them, and turns them into the keys of Strata.  The modifier keys are Shift,
Ctrl and Alt, which is the modifier that the keyboard map calls Mod1.
.table 20 40
Key | What the program makes of it
{k:Up}, {k:Down}, | The key, with Shift, Ctrl and Alt as held.  The
 {k:Left}, {k:Right}, | keys of the keypad with the same names, when Num
 {k:Home}, {k:End}, | Lock is off, are the same keys.
 {k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn}, |
 {k:Ins}, {k:Del} |
{k:Esc} | {k:Esc}; Shift, Ctrl and Alt make no difference.
{k:Backspace} | {k:Backspace}; Alt is kept, so {k:Alt-Backspace}
  | deletes a word; Shift and Ctrl are dropped, so
  | {k:Ctrl-Backspace} deletes a character.
{k:Tab} | A tab; with Shift, {k:Shift-Tab}.  Ctrl and Alt
  | make no difference.
{k:Enter}, | {k:Enter}, whatever is held with it.
 {k:Keypad Enter} |
{k:F1} to {k:F12} | The key, with Shift, Ctrl and Alt as held.  The
  | Editor uses {k:F1} to {k:F9} and {k:F12}; {k:Ctrl-F10} and
  | {k:Ctrl-F11} are Undo and Redo (so are {k:Ctrl-/} and {k:Alt-/}), and {k:F10} and {k:F11} have no other
  | command.
{k:Keypad +}, | {k:Alt-+}, {k:Alt--} and {k:Alt-*}, with Num Lock
 {k:Keypad -}, | on or off: the outline keys.  They never type a
 {k:Keypad *} | plus, a minus or a star; type those with the keys
  | of the main keyboard.
Keypad digits, | The character, when Num Lock is on.
 {k:Keypad /} and the |
 decimal point |
A letter, digit or sign | The character.  With Alt it is {k:Alt} and the
  | character, in the case that is typed ({k:Alt-O}
  | and {k:Alt-o} are the same command).  With Ctrl it
  | is the control key; with Ctrl and Alt together it
  | is {k:Alt} and the control key.
A letter of Latin-1 | A letter of Latin-1 that is in the character set
  | is typed as that letter ({n:appx11-chars}).
{k:Shift}, {k:Ctrl}, | By themselves they give no key.  The program only
 {k:Alt}, Meta, | notes the modifier keys, for the labels of the
 {k:Caps Lock}, | function keys.
 {k:Num Lock}, |
 {k:Scroll Lock}, Super, |
 AltGr |
.end

.note The key labels at the foot of the screen change while a modifier is held
  ({n:view-status}).  When the window loses the focus the labels go back to the
  plain ones.

.h3 Characters [appx11-chars]
.ix Characters, X11
.ix Latin-1
.ix Dead keys
.ix Compose key
A key that produces a letter of Latin-1 that the character set has is typed as
that letter: the accented letters of the western languages that are in the
set, and the German double s, among others.  The keys of a keyboard map that
give the accented letter at once (for instance on the level of the right Alt
key of many layouts) work.  What happens with a letter that the set does not
have, and with dead keys and Compose sequences, is described in
{x:edit-special-chars}; the characters are listed in {x:appchar-high}.

.h2 Keyboard maps and window managers [appx11-keymap]
.ix Keymap
.ix Keymap, problems
.ix Window manager, keys
.ix Alt-F12
.ix F12, wrong menu
.ix xev
.ix xmodmap
The Editor uses the function keys with Alt and Ctrl, which a keyboard map
or a window manager can spoil.  The program takes the keys as the server
delivers them; the only remedy is to change what the server delivers.  The
program xev, which shows each key event with its state, is the tool to find
out; the modifier Mod1 is Alt, and Control is Ctrl.
