.h2 The size of the window [appx11-size]
.ix Window size
.ix Rows and columns, X11
.ix Resize, X11
The window is as big as its grid: the number of columns times the width of a
cell, the number of rows times the height of a cell.  The grid is 80 columns
by 50 rows, or the number of rows that you kept (see below).
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.ix STRATA_X11_GEOM
.table 16 44
Name | Effect
{c:STRATA_X11_GEOM} | {c:COLSxROWS}, for example {c:100x40}.  Fewer
  | than 20 columns are made 20, fewer than 20 rows
  | are made 20. A value that is not two numbers
  | leaves the grid as it was.  When it is set, the
  | rows that you kept are not used and a change of
  | the height is not kept.
{c:STRATA_X11_SIZE} | The size of the font, in pixels
  | ({n:appx11-fonts}).  Together with the family it
  | fixes the size of a cell.
.end
.endif
You may change the height of the window with the window manager: drag its
lower edge or its corner.  The height changes in whole rows, from 20 rows to as
many as the screen holds, and the Editor follows it as the terminal version
follows its terminal: the text is laid out again, the status line stays at the
top and the key labels stay at the foot.  The width cannot be changed: a width
that the window manager gives all the same is not used.  The text is laid out
in at most 80 columns and at least 40, whatever the grid.  A window needs the
room that its grid takes on the screen.  A cell is higher than the pixel size
of the font, so a grid of 50 rows does not fit a screen that is 768 pixels
high with the default font: use fewer rows (for example 80 by 35) or a smaller
font, or make the window lower.  Boxes and panels are as in the terminal
version ({n:appterm-size}); the tallest, the Typeface List, needs about 24 rows.
.h3 The height is kept [appx11-size-kept]
.ix Window height, kept
.ix Height of the window, kept
The Editor and the Document Manager keep the height that you give the window.
When the height has not changed for a second, and at the latest when the
program ends, the number of rows is saved in your settings, and the next window
of either program opens with that height.  There is one height for the
Editor, not one for each document, and the Document Manager has the same
one.  The height is never more than the rows that the screen holds.  You may
also type the number of rows in Setup, in the field Window Rows, X11
({n:str-setup-fields}); an empty field means 50 rows.  A height that cannot
be saved is used until the program ends, and nothing is shown on the screen.
The width is always 80 columns and is not kept.  The terminal version has a
number of its own ({n:appterm-size}).

.h2 The mouse [appx11-mouse]
.ix Mouse
.ix Wheel
.ix Click, cursor
The window takes two things from the mouse, and nothing else.  The terminal
version does not use the mouse at all: no mouse report is requested from
the terminal, so the selection and the paste of the terminal work as they do in
any other program.
.table 22 38
Mouse | What is done
Wheel | The text scrolls by three rows for each step, and by half a
  | screen when {k:Ctrl} is held.  The cursor is moved into the
  | rows that are shown, so that the next key does not bring the
  | view back.  In a list, in a pager and in the help the wheel
  | does what three {k:Up} or {k:Down} keys do ({k:PgUp} and
  | {k:PgDn} with {k:Ctrl}).
Left button | A click in the text puts the cursor on the character
  | under the mouse arrow.  A click after the end of a row puts it at
  | the end of the row; a click on a tab puts it on the tab; a click
  | on a picture puts it on the picture, on the row that was
  | clicked; in a table the cell is found, and below the text of a
  | column that is shorter than the next column the cursor goes to
  | the end of that column.  While text is highlighted, the click
  | moves the end of the highlight, in the same text for a Text or
  | Column highlight, and to any block for a Block or Section
  | highlight.
.end
Everything else is ignored: a click in a menu, in a box that asks for a
choice or for text, on the status line, on the lines that separate the
sections, on the keys at the foot, or in the other window of a split screen;
the other buttons; and the wheel in a menu or in a box that is not a list.
Dragging does not select text, and the double click has no meaning.  The
mouse is not recorded in a macro.  Every command is still on a key, and a
person who uses only the keyboard sees no change.

.h2 Moving text between programs [appx11-clip]
.ix Clipboard
.ix Selection, X11
.ix Copy and paste between programs
.table 25 35
To move text | Use
Between places of one document | Copy, Cut, Paste and Move ({k:F3}, {k:F4},
 or of two documents of the | {k:F5}, {k:F7}): the text is kept in the
 same session | program.
Between sessions and documents | The library ({k:Ctrl-F3}, {k:Ctrl-F4},
 over time | {k:Ctrl-F5}), which Tools, Library, Save
  | writes to a file.
Out of Strata to another | File, Export: a text file of the document or
 program | of the section, which any program reads.
Into Strata from another | Save the text in a file with that program
 program | and use File, Import.
.end
In the X11 version the selection and the clipboard of the display are not
used, and dragging with the mouse selects nothing ({n:appx11-mouse}).  In the terminal version the paste of the terminal types the text as
keys ({n:appterm-chars}).

.h2 Equations and keycaps on the screen [appx11-pictures]
.ix Equations, X11
.ix Keycaps, X11
.ix Pictures on the screen
.ix Ctrl-W, equation
The X11 version draws a marker as a picture when the display mode of its type
is Inline, which is the default of the Equation, the Picture, the Keycap and
the LaTeX marker ({n:markers-screen}).  An Equation marker that is a valid
formula is typeset when {c:latex} and {c:dvipng} were found at the start, and
drawn in the colors of the window, in the line of the text.  The Picture, the
Keycap and the LaTeX marker are drawn the same way when {c:pdflatex} and
{c:pdftoppm} were found ({n:marker-picture}, {n:marker-keycap},
{n:marker-latex-picture}).  While a picture is being made the cells show the
label of the marker.  A picture that cannot be made is shown in the Short form
of the marker, never as an empty box.  {k:Ctrl-W} in the equation
editor (the status line shows EQUATION EDIT) shows an enlarged picture of the
equation, or the message that {c:latex} gave when it could not typeset it; it
needs the X11 version, {c:latex} and {c:dvipng}, and {k:Esc} goes back to the
Editor.
The pictures are kept in the cache of the user ({n:appcfg-search-cache}).  See
{x:eq-screen} for the details.

.h2 The Viewer [appx11-viewer]
.ix Viewer
.ix Viewer, status bar
.ix Preview, viewer
.ix Zoom
.ix Go to page
The Viewer is the program that shows the pages of Preview ({n:print-dviview}).
The Editor starts the Viewer, waits for it, and takes its window back when the
Viewer is closed; the Viewer runs under X11 only.  Its window is as large as the
page at 100 percent zoom and the status bar, but not larger than the screen
(not smaller than 100 by 100 pixels): a page that does not fit is scrolled.
The window can be resized, and the page and the bar follow it.  The title of
the window is the name of the file shown.
.h3 The status bar [appx11-viewer-bar]
.ix Viewer, bar
.ix Viewer, legend
At the foot of the window a grey bar has two lines.  The first one shows
{c:Page N of M   Zoom Z%   F1 or ? hides keys}; while a page number is being
typed the line shows {c:Go to page (1-M): digits_   Enter goes, Esc cancels}.
The
second line is the legend of the keys, in one of four forms, the longest that
fits the width of the window; it is hidden and shown again with {k:F1} or
{k:?}, and then the bar has one line only.
.h3 The keys of the Viewer [appx11-viewer-keys]
.ix Viewer, key table
The keys are in {x:print-dviview}.  The key {k:g} opens the
entry for a page number in the bar: type up to eight digits, {k:Backspace}
deletes one, {k:Enter} goes (a number out of range is brought into range) and
{k:Esc} cancels; in the entry no other key does anything.  The wheel of the
mouse pans the page by the step of {k:Up} and {k:Down}.  The zoom runs from
25 to 800 percent and keeps the center of the window where it was.  A new
page starts at the top left.  Closing the window with the window manager also
closes the Viewer.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the options of the Viewer and its exit status are in {n:appcmd}
({n:appcmd-dviview-opt}, {n:appcmd-dviview-exit}).
.endif
.h3 What the Viewer needs [appx11-viewer-needs]
.ix Viewer, requirements
.ix TrueColor
The display must be a TrueColor display of 32 bits per pixel.  When it is not,
the Viewer writes {c:a 32 bits per pixel display is needed} or {c:unsupported
pixel format} on the standard error and ends.  The programs
that the Viewer needs for the fonts of the pages and for pictures are in
{x:appcmd-external}.
.note When the Editor and the Viewer run on a display of another machine (a
  remote X connection), the Editor works well over a slow link; the Viewer
  needs a fast link.
